=== dcx
dcx is a CLI that parses, validates, and runs devcontainers
with full support for docker compose and Features.

It drives the docker and docker compose CLIs directly, without the
@devcontainers/cli, and tracks all state in container labels. Sessions go
over SSH to a small agent inside the container: use 'dcx ssh' or plain
'ssh <alias>'.

Usage:
  dcx [command]

Lifecycle Commands:
  down        Stop and remove containers
  restart     Restart the devcontainer
  stop        Stop running containers
  up          Start the devcontainer environment

Execution Commands:
  run         Run a command shortcut in the container
  ssh         Open a shell or run a command in the container

Information Commands:
  list        List all dcx-managed environments
  logs        View container logs
  status      Show devcontainer status

Build & Maintenance:
  build       Build the devcontainer images
  clean       Clean up orphaned resources
  lock        Generate or verify devcontainer-lock.json
  upgrade     Upgrade dcx to the latest version

Utilities:
  completion  Generate shell completion scripts
  config      Show devcontainer configuration
  doctor      Check system requirements and configuration

Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

Use "dcx [command] --help" for more information about a command.

=== dcx build
Build the devcontainer images without starting containers.

This command parses the devcontainer.json configuration and builds
any required images. For compose-based configurations, it runs
'docker compose build'. For image-based configurations, it pulls
the image. For Dockerfile-based configurations, it builds the image.

This command may require network access for pulling base images.

Usage:
  dcx build [flags]

Flags:
      --no-cache[=true|false]   build without using cache
      --pull[=true|false]       re-pull base images and re-fetch features (for moving tags like :latest)

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx clean
Clean up orphaned dcx resources.

This command removes:
- Images built by dcx (dcx/*) that no container uses
- What dcx keeps in ~/.dcx (host key, lock, compose override) for
  workspaces that have no container
- Optionally, dangling (untagged) images

By default, derived images and workspace state are cleaned. Use --all to
include dangling images.

This is an offline-safe command that does not require network access.

Usage:
  dcx clean [flags]

Flags:
      --all[=true|false]        also clean dangling images
      --current[=true|false]    only clean images of the current workspace
      --dangling[=true|false]   only clean dangling images
      --dry-run[=true|false]    show what would be cleaned without removing

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx completion
Generate shell completion scripts for dcx.

To load completions:

Bash:
  $ source <(dcx completion bash)

  # To load completions for each session, execute once:
  # Linux:
  $ dcx completion bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/dcx
  # macOS:
  $ dcx completion bash > $(brew --prefix)/etc/bash_completion.d/dcx

Zsh:
  # If shell completion is not already enabled in your environment,
  # you will need to enable it. You can execute the following once:
  $ echo "autoload -U compinit; compinit" >> ~/.zshrc

  # To load completions for each session, execute once:
  $ dcx completion zsh > "${fpath[1]}/_dcx"

  # You may need to start a new shell for this setup to take effect.

Fish:
  $ dcx completion fish | source

  # To load completions for each session, execute once:
  $ dcx completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/dcx.fish

PowerShell:
  PS> dcx completion powershell | Out-String | Invoke-Expression

  # To load completions for every new session, run:
  PS> dcx completion powershell > dcx.ps1
  # and source this file from your PowerShell profile.

Usage:
  dcx completion [bash|zsh|fish|powershell]

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx config
Show the resolved devcontainer.json configuration as JSON (always; there
is no --json flag).

By default, shows devcontainer.json after variable substitution (the base
image's metadata is not merged in; 'dcx status' shows the resolved plan).
Use --raw to show it as written.

Usage:
  dcx config [flags]

Examples:
  dcx config                # Show the resolved config
  dcx config --raw          # Show the config as written
  dcx config --validate     # Only validate the config (no output)

Flags:
      --raw[=true|false]        show original config without variable substitution
      --validate[=true|false]   only validate configuration (no output)

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx doctor
Check that dcx can run here.

System checks: the Docker daemon (and whether you may use it), the docker
compose plugin, and whether Docker labels containers for SELinux.

Configuration checks (when a devcontainer.json is found, or with --config):
the config resolves exactly as 'dcx up' resolves it, and the files it
references exist. When the workspace's container is running, the SSH
checks probe its agent: the port is recorded, accepts connections, and
known_hosts pins the host key.

Usage:
  dcx doctor [flags]

Flags:
      --config-only[=true|false]   only check configuration (skip system checks)
      --json[=true|false]          print machine-readable JSON instead of text
      --system[=true|false]        only check system requirements (skip config checks)

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx down
Stop and remove devcontainer containers.

This is an offline-safe command that stops and removes containers
managed by dcx. Optionally removes volumes and orphan containers.

Usage:
  dcx down [flags]

Flags:
      --remove-orphans[=true|false]   remove containers not defined in compose file
      --volumes[=true|false]          remove named volumes

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx list
List all devcontainer environments managed by dcx.

By default, shows running environments grouped by workspace.
Use --all to include stopped environments.

Examples:
  dcx list              # List running environments
  dcx list --all        # List all environments (including stopped)

Usage:
  dcx list [flags]

Aliases:
  list, ls, ps

Flags:
      --all[=true|false]    show all environments (including stopped)
      --json[=true|false]   print machine-readable JSON (stable keys) instead of a table

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx lock
Generate or verify a lockfile that pins exact feature versions for reproducible builds.

The lockfile has the official devcontainer CLI's format. For each feature,
keyed by its reference as written, it records:
- Exact semantic version resolved
- The resolved reference, pinned to the OCI manifest digest
- Integrity: the manifest digest (or a tarball's SHA256)
- Hard dependencies (dependsOn)

Modes:
  dcx lock           Generate/update lockfile (default)
  dcx lock --verify  Verify existing lockfile matches resolved features
  dcx lock --frozen  Fail if lockfile doesn't exist or doesn't match (CI mode)

Per the devcontainer specification:
- .devcontainer.json → .devcontainer-lock.json
- devcontainer.json → devcontainer-lock.json
- Local features (./path) are excluded from lockfile

Usage:
  dcx lock [flags]

Flags:
      --frozen[=true|false]   fail if lockfile doesn't exist or doesn't match (CI mode)
      --json[=true|false]     print the result as machine-readable JSON
      --verify[=true|false]   verify lockfile matches resolved features without updating

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx logs
View logs from the devcontainer's primary container.

By default, shows the last 100 lines of logs. Use --follow to stream
new log output in real-time.

Examples:
  dcx logs                # Show last 100 lines
  dcx logs --follow       # Stream logs in real-time
  dcx logs --tail 50      # Show last 50 lines
  dcx logs --timestamps   # Include timestamps

Usage:
  dcx logs [flags]

Flags:
  -f, --follow[=true|false]       follow log output
      --tail string               number of lines to show from the end (use 'all' for all logs) (default "100")
  -t, --timestamps[=true|false]   show timestamps

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx restart
Stop the devcontainer and bring it up again.

The start goes through 'dcx up', so the agent, runtime secrets and
postStartCommand are set up again. If devcontainer.json changed since the
container was created, it is recreated.

If devcontainer.json sets shutdownAction to "none", the container is not
restarted unless --force is given.

--rebuild rebuilds the images and recreates the container (shutdownAction
does not apply to it).

Usage:
  dcx restart [flags]

Flags:
  -f, --force[=true|false]     force restart even if shutdownAction is 'none'
      --rebuild[=true|false]   perform full rebuild instead of restart

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx run
Run a configured command shortcut in the devcontainer.

Shortcuts are defined in devcontainer.json under "customizations.dcx.shortcuts".

Example devcontainer.json:
{
  "name": "myproject",
  "image": "ubuntu",
  "customizations": {
    "dcx": {
      "shortcuts": {
        "rw": "bin/jobs --skip-recurring",
        "r": {"prefix": "rails", "passArgs": true},
        "test": {"prefix": "rails test", "passArgs": true, "description": "Run tests"}
      }
    }
  }
}

Use --list to see all available shortcuts.

Usage:
  dcx run [shortcut] [args...] [flags]

Examples:
  dcx run rw                    # Runs: bin/jobs --skip-recurring
  dcx run r server              # Runs: rails server
  dcx run r console             # Runs: rails console
  dcx run test test/models/     # Runs: rails test test/models/

Flags:
  -l, --list[=true|false]   list available shortcuts

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx ssh
Open an interactive shell in the workspace's devcontainer, or run a
command in it.

dcx finds the workspace from the current directory, checks that its
container is running, then hands over to OpenSSH using the host alias
'dcx up' configured. The session is exactly what 'ssh <alias>' gives
editors and other tools: the remote user, remoteEnv, the probed user
environment, SSH agent forwarding, and postAttachCommand for interactive
shells.

Arguments after '--' keep their quoting:

  dcx ssh                          # interactive shell
  dcx ssh -- npm install
  dcx ssh -- bash -c "echo hello"
  dcx ssh -- git clone git@github.com:user/repo.git

'dcx status' shows the alias and endpoint ('--detailed' adds the host key);
'dcx doctor' probes the agent.

Usage:
  dcx ssh [-- command [args...]] [flags]

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx status
Show the current state of the devcontainer environment.

This command queries Docker for containers managed by dcx and displays
their current state (ABSENT, CREATED, RUNNING, STALE, or BROKEN).

Use --detailed for container details, the SSH endpoint and host key, the
resolved plan and features, why a container is stale, and its dcx labels; --json prints the same as stable
JSON (--detailed --json replaces the old hidden 'debug' command).

This is an offline-safe command that does not require network access.

Usage:
  dcx status [flags]

Flags:
  -d, --detailed[=true|false]   show detailed environment information
      --json[=true|false]       print machine-readable JSON (stable keys) instead of text

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx stop
Stop devcontainer containers without removing them.

This is an offline-safe command that only stops running containers.
The containers and their data are preserved and can be started again
with 'dcx up'.

If the devcontainer.json has shutdownAction set to "none", the container
will not be stopped unless --force is used.

Usage:
  dcx stop [flags]

Flags:
  -f, --force[=true|false]   force stop even if shutdownAction is 'none'

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx up
Start the devcontainer environment, building if necessary.

This command is smart about what it needs to do:
- If containers exist and are up to date, just starts them (offline-safe)
- If containers are stale or missing, performs full build/create sequence
- Use --rebuild to force image rebuild
- Use --recreate to force container recreation

Lifecycle hooks run as appropriate based on the action taken.

Usage:
  dcx up [flags]

Flags:
      --hosts string            widen SSH access beyond loopback when creating the container (e.g. --hosts=10.0.0.0/24, --hosts=any, --hosts=loopback); kept until changed with --recreate
      --pull[=true|false]       re-pull the base image and re-fetch features (for moving tags like :latest); applies when the container is created, so add --recreate for an existing one
      --rebuild[=true|false]    force rebuild images
      --recreate[=true|false]   force recreate containers

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

=== dcx upgrade
Check for and install the latest release of dcx from GitHub.

The binary is verified against the release's checksums.txt and replaced in
place. Nothing happens when this dcx is the latest release or newer; a
development build or a newer build is replaced only with --force, which
also reinstalls the current release.

Usage:
  dcx upgrade [flags]

Flags:
      --force[=true|false]   install the latest release even over a development, newer or identical build

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string           path to devcontainer.json, relative to the current directory (default: auto-detect in the workspace)
      --no-color[=true|false]   disable colored output
  -q, --quiet[=true|false]      print only data and errors (no progress or warnings)
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]    verbose output
  -w, --workspace string        workspace directory (default: current directory)

